QB.add(40,[{"n":1,"q":"A 67-year-old male who previously worked in the petrochemical industry presents with complaints of generalized fatigue, fever and difficulty breathing. On examination, conjunctival pallor and petechiae over the ankle are noted. Bone marrow evaluation reveals 25% myeloblasts. Which of the following is the likely diagnosis? Complete Blood Count (CBC): Hemoglobin: 8.2 g/dL White Blood Cell Count: 1,20,000/µL Platelet Count: 30,000/µL","o":[{"t":"Acute lymphoblastic leukaemia (ALL)"},{"t":"Acute myeloid leukaemia (AML)"},{"t":"Chronic myelocytic leukaemia (CML)"},{"t":"Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia (CLL)"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct answer: B) Acute myeloid leukaemia (AML)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The most likely diagnosis in this case is Acute myeloid leukaemia (AML)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Acute myeloid leukaemia (AML) is a hematologic malignancy characterised by the rapid proliferation of immature myeloid cells (blasts) in the bone marrow."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"AML is the most common type of acute leukaemia in adults. It is typically seen in those older than 65 years of age."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The complete blood count, clinical presentation, and risk factors all align with this diagnosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Elevated White Blood Cell Count:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The markedly elevated white blood cell count (120,000/µL) indicates leukocytosis, which is common in leukemias, including AML."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Presence of Myeloblasts:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The bone marrow evaluation reveals 25% myeloblasts. A diagnosis of acute myeloid leukemia typically requires the presence of at least 20% myeloblasts in the bone marrow or peripheral blood."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cytopenias:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient exhibits anemia (hemoglobin of 8.2 g/dL) and thrombocytopenia (platelet count of 30,000/µL). These cytopenias result from the replacement of normal hematopoietic cells in the bone marrow by malignant myeloid blasts, leading to decreased production of red blood cells and platelets."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Symptoms and Physical Findings:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The symptoms of generalized fatigue and difficulty breathing are consistent with anemia and potential leukostasis. Conjunctival pallor indicates significant anemia, while petechiae suggest thrombocytopenia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Risk Factors:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient’s occupational history in the petrochemical industry indicates exposure to carcinogenic agents, which is a known risk factor for the development of hematologic malignancies, including AML."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Acute lymphoblastic leukaemia (ALL) (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ALL may also show features of pancytopenia, but there is also typically lymphadenopathy. Further, the peripheral smear and bone marrow aspirate show lymphoblasts."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chronic myelocytic leukaemia (CML) (Option C)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CML is commonly asymptomatic. When symptomatic, fatigue, weight loss, excessive sweating, abdominal fullness (due to splenomegaly), and bleeding are seen. Hyperleukocytosis is common, but the bone marrow would show multiple granulocytes, with a maturation pattern resembling a normal peripheral smear."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia (CLL) (Option D)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CLL is commonly an incidental diagnosis in elderly adults. When symptomatic, manifestations include fatigue, lymphadenopathy, and hepatosplenomegaly. While hyperleukocytosis may be seen, the bone marrow would show hypercellularity with lymphocytes."}]},{"n":2,"q":"A 55-year-old female presents with fatigue, weight loss, and easy bruising. A bone marrow aspiration is performed, and the results are shown below. Which of the following is not a cause for this condition?","o":[{"t":"Idiopathic"},{"t":"Downs syndrome"},{"t":"Diamond Black Fann syndrome"},{"t":"NF-1"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option: D) NF-1"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The bone marrow smear of this patient shows several myeloblasts with prominent nucleoli and Auer rods."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the clinical findings (fatigue and easy bruising likely due to cytopenias) and the bone marrow results, the most likely diagnosis is Acute Myeloid Leukemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"NF-1 is not an aetiology of AML. It is rather associated with ALL."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Aetiology of AML:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Idiopathic (25-50% (25-50% cases) (Option A) cases)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Bone Marrow Failure: Fanconi Anemia"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Diamond-Blackfan Syndrome (Option C): Diamond-Blackfan Anemia (DBA) is a rare inherited form of macrocytic anaemia characterised by a failure of erythropoiesis in the bone marrow. Shwachman-Diamond Syndrome: An autosomal recessive disorder involving exocrine pancreatic insufficiency."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Defective DNA Repair: Bloom Syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Ataxia Telangiectasia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Down Syndrome: AML is associated with Down syndrome, particularly the (Option B) subtype known as Acute Megakaryoblastic Leukemia"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(AML M7) , which involves the GATA1 gene."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Exposure to Anticance Alkylating agents -> Monosomy 5 & 7 -> AML (After 4-6 r Drugs: years)."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Anticancer drugs (Topoisomerase 2 inhibitors) -> Chromosome 11 involvement -> AML (After 1-3 years)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Radiation Exposure: Exposure to radiation, benzene, phenylbutazone, and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"chloramphenicol may increase the risk of developing AML and bone marrow failure."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Idiopathic (25-50% (25-50% cases) (Option A) cases)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"t(15:17): Chromosomal translocation results in the PML-RARA"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"fusion gene, which is associated with acute promyelocytic leukaemia (APL), a subtype of AML."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"t(8:21): Chromosomal translocation (RUNX1- RUNX1T1) is"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"associated with myeloid sarcoma."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Inversion 16 or t(16;16)."}],"qi":[6702]},{"n":3,"q":"Match the following appropriately- 1. Acute monocytic leukemia A. M0 2. AML without maturation B. M2 3. Acute myelomonocytic Leukaemia C. M5 4. AML with minimal dinerentiation D. M4 E. M1\n1. Acute monocytic leukemia A. M0\n2. AML without maturation B. M2\n3. Acute myelomonocytic Leukaemia C. M5\n4. AML with minimal dinerentiation D. M4\nE. M1","o":[{"t":"1-C, 2-E,3-D,4-A"},{"t":"1-B, 2-A,3-D,4-E"},{"t":"1-D, 2-E,3-C,4-B"},{"t":"1-D, 2-E,3-A,4-B"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) 1-C, 2-E,3-D,4-A:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The French-American-British (FAB) classification of acute myeloid leukaemia (AML) categorizes AML into subtypes based on the morphology of the blasts and their differentiation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"It includes subtypes like M0 to M7, each representing different stages of myeloid cell development."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"On the other hand, the World Health Organization (WHO) classification further refines AML classification by integrating cytogenetic and molecular genetic information along with morphology."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"FAB (French-American-British) classification for AML:-"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M0 Minimally differentiated leukaemia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M1 Myeloblastic leukaemia without maturation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M2 Myeloblastic leukaemia with maturation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M3 Hypergranular promyelocytic leukemia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M4 Myelomonocytic leukemia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M4E0 (variant) Increase in abnormal marrow eosinophils"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M5 Acute Monocytic leukemia M0 Minimally differentiated leukaemia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M6 Erythroleukemia (DiGuglielmo’s disease)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M7 Megakaryoblastic leukaemia"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"WHO 2016 Classification:-"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) with recurrent genetic abnormalities AML with t(8;21)(q22;q22); RUNX1-RUNX1T1 AML with inv(16)(p13.1q22) or t(16;16)(p13.1;q22); CBFB-MYH11 Acute promyelocytic leukemia with PML-RARA AML with t(9;11)(p21.3;q23.3); MLLT3-KMT2A AML with t(6;9)(p23;q34.1); DEK-NUP214 AML with inv(3)(q21.3q26.2) or t(3;3)(q21.3;q26.2); GATA2, MECOM AML (megakaryoblastic) with t(1;22)(p13.3;q13.3); RBM15-MKL1 Provisional entity: AML with BCR-ABL1 AML with mutated NPM1 AML with biallelic mutations of CEBPA Provisional entity: AML with mutated RUNX1"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"AML with myelodysplasia-related changes Therapy-related myeloid neoplasms"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"AML, not otherwise specified (NOS) AML with minimal differentiation AML without maturation AML with maturation Acute myelomonocytic leukemia Acute monoblastic/monocytic leukemia Pure erythroid leukemia Acute megakaryoblastic leukemia Acute basophilic leukemia Acute panmyelosis with myelofibrosis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Myeloid sarcoma"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Myeloid proliferations related to Down syndrome Transient abnormal myelopoiesis (TAM) Myeloid leukemia associated with Down syndrome"}]},{"n":4,"q":"A 42-year-old female patient presents to the clinic with fatigue, recurrent infections, and easy bruising. Laboratory tests reveal anaemia, thrombocytopenia, and an elevated white blood cell count with a high proportion of blasts. Bone marrow biopsy confirms the diagnosis of acute myeloid leukaemia (AML). Which of the following cytogenetic abnormalities is not a favourable prognosis in acute myeloid leukaemia (AML)?","o":[{"t":"t (8:21)"},{"t":"Inversion of chromosome 16"},{"t":"t (16:16)"},{"t":"FLT3-ITD high"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) FLT3-ITD high"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"FLT3-ITD high typically indicates a high level of the mutation, which is often linked to a more aggressive disease course and poorer prognosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The favorable prognostic features include"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CEBPA"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"NPM1"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"t (8:21) (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inversion of chromosome 16 (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"t (16:16) (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Features associated with poor prognosis include,"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Wild type NPM1 and FLT3-ITD high(c) (Option D)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mutated RUNX1"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mutated TP53"}]},{"n":5,"q":"A 55-year-old man presents with persistent fever, epistaxis, and pallor. Lab investigations are shown below. Hb- 7.3 g/dl WBC count- 75000 cells/mm3 Blast count- 25%, positive for t (15,17) Platelet count- 48000 /cumm What is the most probable diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Acute Myeloid leukemia"},{"t":"Acute lymphoid leukemia"},{"t":"Preleukemia"},{"t":"Chronic lymphoid leukemia"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Acute Myeloid leukaemia"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The given clinical scenario, along with the presence of blast cells and high WBC count, positive t (15,17) suggests Acute myeloblastic leukaemia (AML)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigations for AML:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CBC: Low Hb and platelet count TLC is initially low (Maturational arrest. Later, it increases (Leading to lymphadenopathy and leukostasis)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low Hb and platelet count"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"TLC is initially low (Maturational arrest. Later, it increases (Leading to lymphadenopathy and leukostasis)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low Hb and platelet count"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"TLC is initially low (Maturational arrest. Later, it increases (Leading to lymphadenopathy and leukostasis)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bone marrow aspiration (IOC): Auer rods are seen (Acute promyelocytic leukaemia)"},{"k":"img","v":6718},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Flow cytometry analysis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CD13 and CD117 are the flow cytometry markers for AML."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Megakaryoblastic leukaemia: CD41 and CD61 are the markers. (AML M7 subtype)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Molecular study: FLT3 CEBPA NPM1"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"FLT3"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CEBPA"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"NPM1"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"FLT3"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CEBPA"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"NPM1"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chest X-ray for lymphadenopathy, Echocardiography (Detect Heart failure)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HLA typing (Needed in case of Allogenic blood cell transplantation)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Baseline KFT (To identify manifestations of Tumour lysis syndrome)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"According to WHO, the diagnosis of AML requires one of the following,"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blast cell count of >/= 20%"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blast cell count of < 20% if any of the following chromosomal anomalies are present, t (15,17) t (8,21) t (16,16), inv 16"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"t (15,17)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"t (8,21)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"t (16,16), inv 16"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"t (15,17)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"t (8,21)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"t (16,16), inv 16"}]},{"n":6,"q":"A 28-year-old male patient presents to the emergency department with severe fatigue, unexplained bruising, and nosebleeds. Her complete blood count reveals severe anaemia and thrombocytopenia. The peripheral blood smear image is given. Bone marrow biopsy confirms the diagnosis of acute promyelocytic leukaemia (APL). What is the first-line treatment for acute promyelocytic leukaemia (APL)?","o":[{"t":"Induction chemotherapy with venetoclax"},{"t":"All-trans retinoic acid (ATRA) therapy"},{"t":"Allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HCT)"},{"t":"Supportive care with transfusions"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct answer: B) All-trans retinoic acid (ATRA) therapy"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above given clinical scenario is the M3 subtype of AML- Acute promyelocytic leukaemia (APL)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Seen in young patients with chromosomal translocation t (15,17)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"M3 AML is associated with PML-RARA mutation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The initial treatment is ATRA (All trans-retinoic acid) ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ATRA works by inducing the differentiation of promyelocytes into mature granulocytes, leading to the resolution of the leukemic process. It is typically used in combination with anthracycline-based chemotherapy, such as daunorubicin or idarubicin."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ATRA can cause differentiation syndrome (Damage of pulmonary endothelium by cytokines released from differentiated cells causing respiratory distress), treated with Steroids and supplemental oxygen."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Induction chemotherapy for AML:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cytarabine + Anthracyclines (Daunorubicin or Idarubicin)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anthracyclines can cause cardiotoxicity."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Induction chemotherapy with venetoclax (Option A): Induction chemotherapy with venetoclax is a treatment option for older patients > 65 years with AML, particularly those who cannot tolerate standard intensive chemotherapy or have specific genetic mutations."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HCT) (Option C): While allogeneic HCT may be considered in some cases of AML, such as high-risk or relapsed/refractory disease, it is typically reserved for consolidation therapy after achieving remission or for salvage therapy in cases of relapse. It is not usually part of the initial treatment for AML."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Supportive care with transfusions (Option D) : Supportive care, including transfusions for anaemia and platelet transfusions for bleeding, is important in the management of AML, but it is not the primary treatment for the disease itself. It is typically given alongside specific anti-leukemic therapy."}],"qi":[6707]},{"n":7,"q":"A 72-year-old female is recently diagnosed with AML. Her WBC count is 1.50 lakh/mm3. Which of the following organs is most commonly affected in symptomatic hyperleukocytosis?","o":[{"t":"Liver"},{"t":"Brain"},{"t":"Kidney"},{"t":"Bowel"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Brain"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyperleukocytosis refers to a WBC count >1 lakh/mm3 in leukaemia . While common in all types of leukaemia, symptomatic hyperleukocytosis/ leucostasis is most often seen in AML."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms occur due to capillary plugging, endothelial damage, and oxygen consumption by WBCs, causing end-organ damage."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The most commonly involved organs are the brain (approx 40%), followed by the lungs (approx. 30%). CNS - Visual changes, headache, dizziness, tinnitus, gait instability, confusion, somnolence, coma, increased risk of intracranial haemorrhage. Lung - Dyspnea, hypoxia Other organs (uncommon) - Myocardial ischemia, renal insufficiency, priapism, acute limb ischemia, or bowel infarction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CNS - Visual changes, headache, dizziness, tinnitus, gait instability, confusion, somnolence, coma, increased risk of intracranial haemorrhage."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lung - Dyspnea, hypoxia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other organs (uncommon) - Myocardial ischemia, renal insufficiency, priapism, acute limb ischemia, or bowel infarction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CNS - Visual changes, headache, dizziness, tinnitus, gait instability, confusion, somnolence, coma, increased risk of intracranial haemorrhage."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lung - Dyspnea, hypoxia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other organs (uncommon) - Myocardial ischemia, renal insufficiency, priapism, acute limb ischemia, or bowel infarction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis is clinical-suggestive manifestations along with hyperleukocytosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is an emergency with high mortality rates."}]},{"n":8,"q":"Match the following: 1. DIC A. M5- AML 2. Gingival hyperplasia B. M3- APML 3. Paraplegia and u/l proptosis C. M7- AML 4. Down syndrome D. t( 8:21)\n1. DIC A. M5- AML\n2. Gingival hyperplasia B. M3- APML\n3. Paraplegia and u/l proptosis C. M7- AML\n4. Down syndrome D. t( 8:21)","o":[{"t":"1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C"},{"t":"1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A"},{"t":"1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B"},{"t":"1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-D"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) 1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DIC is more common in M3- APML. (1)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"M3 subtype of AML- Acute promyelocytic leukaemia (APL)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Seen in young patients with chromosomal translocation t (15,17)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"M3 AML is associated with PML-RARA mutation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The initial treatment is ATRA (All trans-retinoic acid) ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ATRA works by inducing the differentiation of promyelocytes into mature granulocytes, leading to the resolution of the leukemic process. It is typically used in combination with anthracycline-based chemotherapy, such as daunorubicin or idarubicin."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gingival hyperplasia is commonly seen in M5- AML.(2)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"FAB (French-American-British) classification for AML:-"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M0 Minimally differentiated leukaemia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M1 Myeloblastic leukaemia without maturation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M2 Myeloblastic leukaemia with maturation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M3 Hypergranular promyelocytic leukemia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M4 Myelomonocytic leukemia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M4E0 (variant) Increase in abnormal marrow eosinophils"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M5 Acute Monocytic leukemia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M6 Erythroleukemia (DiGuglielmo’s disease)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M7 Megakaryoblastic leukaemia"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Paraplegia and U/L proptosis are commonly seen with t(8:21) . This genetic alteration can lead to the development of chloroma, also known as Granulocytic sarcoma, which is a type of extramedullary manifestation of AML.(3)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"AML is associated with Down syndrome , particularly the subtype known as Acute Megakaryoblastic Leukemia (AML M7) , which involves the GATA1 gene. (4)"}]},{"n":9,"q":"A 45-year-old male patient came to the clinic with complaints of progressive fatigue and weight loss over the past 3 months. On physical examination, pallor and an orbital swelling leading to proptosis are seen. Bone marrow analysis showed hypercellularity with the predominance of myeloblasts and t(8,21) on FISH. All are true regarding this condition except:","o":[{"t":"Caused due to extra-medullary blast proliferation"},{"t":"Swelling is very painful"},{"t":"Represents poor prognosis"},{"t":"Treatment includes anthracycline-based chemotherapy."}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Swelling is very painful"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient's history of fatigue and weight loss, combined with the physical finding of pallor and proptosis, raises suspicion for an underlying hematological disorder."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bone marrow analysis revealing hypercellularity with a predominance of myeloblasts, confirms the diagnosis of Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"FISH analysis identifying the presence of the t(8;21) translocation, which is commonly associated with a subtype of AML, further substantiates the diagnosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of orbital swelling in the context of AML and t(8,21) points towards Chloroma."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CHLOROMA"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Definition:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chloroma, also known as myeloid sarcoma or granulocytic sarcoma , is a tumor consisting of myeloid cells that proliferate outside the bone marrow. It is a manifestation of acute myeloid leukemia (AML). (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Presentation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Common symptoms include swelling or masses in various locations (e.g., orbit, soft tissues, skin). Swelling is typically painless and green in color. (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients experience proptosis if the chloroma is located in the orbit, or neurological deficits if it compresses the spinal cord."},{"k":"img","v":6727},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Aetiology:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chloromas are often associated with AML but can occur in the absence of leukemia (de novo) as well."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The condition is linked to the presence of t(8;21) translocations."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Prognosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of chloromas generally indicates a poorer prognosis due to the aggressive nature of the underlying disease . (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis is typically confirmed through imaging (CT, MRI) to identify the mass and biopsy to establish the myeloid lineage of the cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"C ytogenetic analysis (e.g., FISH) can identify associated genetic abnormalities."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Treatment usually involves systemic chemotherapy , often using anthracycline-based regimens . (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Differential Diagnosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is essential to differentiate chloroma from other types of tumors or masses, including lymphoma, sarcoma, and other hematological malignancies."}]},{"n":10,"q":"A 52-year-old female presents to the emergency department with complaints of sudden onset bruising, petechiae, and frequent nose bleeds. She reports increasing fatigue and shortness of breath over the past month. On examination, conjunctival pallor and multiple ecchymoses on her arms and legs are noted. A bone marrow biopsy shows hypercellularity with 27% of myeloblasts. Laboratory tests reveal: Haemoglobin: 7.5 g/dL Platelet Count: 20,000/µL White Blood Cell Count: 75,000/µL . PT: 15 seconds (normal: 11-13 seconds) PTT: 37 seconds (normal: 25-35 seconds) Fibrinogen: 100 mg/dL (low) D-dimer: Elevated (>10,000 ng/mL) What type of Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is most commonly associated with this patient’s presentation?","o":[{"t":"M3 AML"},{"t":"M2 AML"},{"t":"M5 AML"},{"t":"M7 AML"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) M3 AML"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient’s clinical presentation, examination findings, bone marrow analysis, and laboratory tests align with AML presenting with DIC."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical presentation: Bruising, petechiae, frequent nose bleeds, conjunctival pallor- seen in AML due to anaemia and thrombocytopenia (substantiated by CBC) seen in AML."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Coagulation profile reveals elevated PT, PTT, and D- dimer levels and decreased fibrinogen levels as seen in DIC."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bone marrow biopsy showing hypercellularity with 27% of myeloblasts (>25% of blasts is required for the diagnosis of AML) confirms the diagnosis of AML."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"DIC is more common in M3- APML."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"M3 subtype of AML- Acute promyelocytic leukaemia (APL)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Granules in promyelocytes contain thromboplastin which activates the coagulation cascade leading to DIC."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Seen in young patients with chromosomal translocation t (15,17)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"M3 AML is associated with PML-RARA mutation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The initial treatment is ATRA (All trans-retinoic acid) ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ATRA works by inducing the differentiation of promyelocytes into mature granulocytes, leading to the resolution of the leukemic process. It is typically used in combination with anthracycline-based chemotherapy, such as daunorubicin or idarubicin."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CD13 and CD33 are the immunophenotype markers of promyelocytes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Faggot cells ( due to clustering of Auer rods) are seen on bone marrow analysis or peripheral smear."},{"k":"img","v":6735},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Faggot cell with numerous Auer rods."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Overall, it has a good prognosis with treatment."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"FAB (French-American-British) classification for AML:-"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M0 Minimally differentiated leukaemia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M1 Myeloblastic leukaemia without maturation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M2 (Option B) Myeloblastic leukaemia with maturation"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Most common type of AML Better prognosis compared to other types"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M0 Minimally differentiated leukaemia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M3 Hypergranular promyelocytic leukemia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M4 Myelomonocytic leukemia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M4E0 (variant) Increase in abnormal marrow eosinophils"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M5 (Option C) Acute Monocytic leukemia"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Gum infiltration Skin involvement- Leukemia cutis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M6 Erythroleukemia (DiGuglielmo’s disease)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"M7 (Option D) Megakaryoblastic leukaemia"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Associated with Down syndrome Involves GATA1 gene"}]}]);
